name:In Processionibus extraordinariis: Pro petenda pluvia (versus et oratio);
office-part:Versiculus, Oratio;
mode:;
book:Processionarium O.P. (Cormier), 1913, p. 133 (text, with the flexa signs and colons as printed; the prayer ends the series begun with Orémus. Concéde nos, p. 132, and is concluded Per Christum Dóminum nostrum as that rubric prescribes);
commentary:V. and R.: Versicle: Art. II § IV 3° (pp. 395-396: "Subscripto modo dicantur Versiculi ad Memorias et ante Laudes, et in Processionibus, et quotiescumque dicitur V. a Sacerdote"): tenor h, the syllables after the last accent of the versicle fall to f, as in the printed example Dignáre me laudáre te, Virgo sacráta. R. Da mihi virtútem contra hostes tuos (pp. 395-396). Proparoxytone endings: both syllables after the accent fall (geminatur nota re, p. 402). The fall is where pp. 131-134 print the flexa sign. F clef on the 3rd line as printed. Prayer (said without Orémus, which went before Concéde nos): Prayer: Art. III § III, pp. 407-408 ("Modus cantandi Orationes ad Horas, et quotiescumque aliquae aliae Orationes quae non sunt de corpore Officii et Missae cantandae fuerint"), applied after its printed example Concéde nos fámulos tuos (p. 407) and conclusions (p. 408): Orémus h h f, tenor h, at the flexa sign of the text the syllables after the last accent fall to f, at the colon (metrum) the two syllables before the last accent (three if they end a dactyl, Art. III § II 2°, p. 405) on g, the last of them on f, the accent and the rest on h, R. Amen g h (p. 401, VII 2°, p. 408). The inflexions fall where the book marks them on pp. 132-134. F clef on the 3rd line as printed.;
transcriber:the tone printed in the Processionarium O.P. 1913 applied to this text for this site (the book prints this text without notes);
%%
